!! Q: The LLM cannot read or modify a specific tiddler. Why?

The tiddler is likely matched by the protection filter. By default, all tiddlers tagged `sensitive` and all rimir plugin internals (`$:/plugins/rimir/*`) are hidden from the LLM. Check Settings > Protection Filter to see the active filter and adjust it if needed. You can also add per-chat protection via the shield icon.

!! Q: Can I use llm-connect with a single-file wiki (no server)?

Yes. llm-connect is entirely browser-side — it makes direct XHR calls to the LLM provider API from the browser. No TiddlyWiki server is required. You only need a valid API key and a modern browser with CORS support (all major LLM APIs support browser-origin requests).

!! Q: Can I use several providers or several accounts at once?

Yes. Add one connection per provider or account in Settings > Connections and give each a name. Pick the connection per chat (the selector in the chat header, until the first message) or per action with `<$llm-action connection="Azure OpenAI" .../>`; the default connection is used otherwise.

!! Q: How does tier routing choose a model?

Each connection has optional ''cheap-model'' and ''expensive-model'' fields. When tier routing is on (Routing tab), tool rounds switch to those models within the chat's own connection. Leave a field empty to keep the chat's model for that tier.

!! Q: I upgraded and my provider settings are gone.

See ''Upgrading from 0.1.56'' in the reference: provider config tiddlers are replaced by connection tiddlers.

!! Q: How do I attach a PDF to a conversation?

Use the context filter (paperclip icon in the chat panel). Enter a filter that matches tiddlers with `_canonical_uri` pointing to PDFs, e.g., `[title[My Document.pdf]]`. The file resolver detects the `_canonical_uri`, fetches the file via XHR, base64-encodes it, and sends it to the LLM as a document attachment. Maximum file size is configurable (default 20 MB).

!! Q: How do I connect a self-hosted model (llama.cpp, vLLM, Ollama, LM Studio)?

Add a connection of type ''OpenAI-compatible'' (Settings > Connections) and set its endpoint to the server's base URL (e.g. `http://gpu-host:8080`; a trailing `/v1` is optional). Set the API key only if the server requires one (llama.cpp `--api-key` / `--api-key-file`). Then click ''Fetch models''. Things to check on the server side:

* ''Tool use'' — llama.cpp needs `--jinja`, otherwise tool calls are not returned as structured `tool_calls`.
* ''CORS'' — requests come straight from the browser, so the server (or a reverse proxy in front of it) must accept the wiki's origin, including the `OPTIONS` preflight.
* ''Mixed content'' — a wiki served over `https` cannot call an `http` endpoint.
* ''Reasoning models'' — inline `<think>…</think>` blocks (Qwen3, DeepSeek-R1) are stripped from replies. Reasoning still consumes output tokens, so raise ''Max tokens'' if replies get cut off.
* ''Documents'' — local servers generally can't read raw PDF/DOCX attachments; keep document extraction enabled so they are sent as text.

